Candidate thresholds function:
```
def threshold_grid(scores):
    u=np.unique(scores)
    if len(u)==1: return np.array([0.0, 1.0, u[0]])
    return np.unique(np.concatenate(([0.0], (u[:-1]+u[1:])/2, [u[-1]+1e-6], [1.0])))
```

select_max_f2:
```
best=(-1,None)
for t in grid:
  pred=scores>=t
  f2=fbeta_score(...)
  if f2 > best + 1e-12: ...
```

select_max_recall_at_precision_ge(y,scores,min_precision=0.8):
```
best=(-1,-1,None)
for t:
  pred...
  rec=recall; prec=precision
  if prec +1e-12 >= min_precision:
     if rec > best_rec +1e-12 or (abs(rec-best_rec)<=1e-12 and prec>best_prec): ...
if None: return scores.min()-1e-6 (all positive) with fallback flag
```
At t=0 all positive, precision=prevalence=0.7786 <0.8, so not eligible. There will be some threshold with precision>=0.8 likely.

select_recall_at_pp_budget:
```
for t grid:
  pp=int((scores>=t).sum())
  if pp <= budget:
     rec=recall
     choose max rec; tie-break higher precision then higher threshold? 
```
At t=0, pp=140 > 120; as t rises pp decreases. Choose max recall under budget. Note threshold grid includes 1.0 pp=0 → recall 0. Monotonic pp decreasing with threshold, recall nonincreasing. So optimum is boundary. Tie-break prefer higher precision.

Important: selecting threshold on val using grid includes t=0 and t=1.0. The `select_threshold` function from train handles.

